---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../styles/mex.scss";
import bg from "../../assets/images/mex-bg.svg"
import clientLogo from "../../assets/images/rki/logo.png"
import userJourney from "../../assets/images/rki/userJourney.png"
import annotations from "../../assets/images/rki/annotations.png";
import improvements from "../../assets/images/rki/improvements.png";
import landingPage from "../../assets/images/rki/landingPage.png";
import mexCarriedContext from "../../assets/images/rki/mexCarriedContext.png";
import mexSelectedDatasets from "../../assets/images/rki/mexSelectedDatasets.png";
import missingData from "../../assets/images/rki/missingData.png";
import recordsRelationship from "../../assets/images/rki/recordsRelationship.png";
import settings from "../../assets/images/rki/settings.png";
import {Image} from "astro:assets";

const project = {
    client: "MEx · Robert Koch Institute",
    role: "Product designer & project manager",
    contributions: [
        "Research",
        "UX/UI design",
        "Client facilitation",
        "Front-end development",
        "Prioritisation and delivery"
    ],
    team: "Four-person Cottage Labs team working with the RKI client team",
    period: "2025 – present",
    status: "Implemented, ongoing development and maintenance",
};
---

<BaseLayout title="MEx Metadata Explorer" bg={bg}>
    <main class="container case-study case-study--mex">
        <article class="case-study">
            <div class="hero hero--project hero--project--mex">
                <p class="hero--project__client">
                    <span>{project.client}</span>
                    <Image src={clientLogo} format="webp"
                           alt="Robert Koch Institute"
                           class="client-logo"/>
                </p>

                <h1 class="hero--project__description easy-read">
                    Helping researchers find the right data across more than 130,000 interconnected records
                </h1>

                <p class="hero--project__summary easy-read">
                    MEx is a metadata catalogue for the Robert Koch Institute, Germany’s national public health
                    institute. It helps researchers discover relevant datasets, variables, projects and
                    publications—including data that cannot be made publicly available.
                </p>

                <dl class="project-meta">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="sr-only">Roles</dt>
                        <dd class="tag project-meta--role">{project.role}</dd>
                    </div>
                    <div class="project-meta--contributions">
                        <dt class="sr-only">Contributions</dt>
                        {project.contributions.map((contribution) => (
                                <dd class="tag">{contribution}</dd>
                        ))}
                    </div>
                    <div class="project-meta--other">
                        <div class="project-meta--other__item">
                            <dt>Period:</dt>
                            <dd>{project.period}</dd>
                        </div>
                        <div class="project-meta--other__item">
                            <dt>Team:</dt>
                            <dd>{project.team}</dd>
                        </div>
                        <div class="project-meta--other__item">
                            <dt>Status:</dt>
                            <dd>{project.status}</dd>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </dl>
            </div>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Making complex metadata navigable</h2>

                    <p>
                        Before researchers request access to a dataset, they need to know whether it could answer their
                        question. Because much public-health data cannot be made openly available, they must make that
                        judgement from its metadata: what was collected, which populations and time periods it covers,
                        which
                        variables it contains and who can provide access.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        At MEx’s scale, this becomes a substantial information-architecture challenge. More than 130,000
                        multilingual records connect datasets with variables, projects, publications, people and
                        organisational units. Those relationships provide valuable context, but each one also creates
                        another route through which researchers could lose track of their investigation.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        As product designer and project manager, I shaped this experience from discovery through
                        delivery. I
                        interviewed users, worked with RKI’s metadata experts to clarify requirements and priorities,
                        designed the UX and UI, coordinated the four-person Cottage Labs team and contributed to the
                        front-end implementation.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <figure class="case-study-figure case-study-figure--mex-selected-datasets">
                    <Image src={mexSelectedDatasets} format="webp"
                           alt="MEx search interface showing results alongside a panel of selected datasets."
                           class=""/>
                    <figcaption class="board-note">
                        The search design keeps promising datasets available as researchers refine their query and move
                        between dataset and variable searches. All prototypes shown in this case study use fictional
                        metadata.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Understanding how researchers work</h2>

                    <p>
                        RKI came to the project with detailed requirements and deep knowledge of its metadata. I
                        interviewed
                        the client team’s metadata expert and team lead—both users of MEx—to understand how researchers
                        assess whether a dataset is worth pursuing and what information they need along the way.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Their workflow is not linear. Researchers begin with a broad search for potentially relevant
                        datasets, then inspect and compare the variables those datasets contain. What they learn during
                        comparison helps them exclude weaker candidates and informs the next dataset search.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        We considered combining dataset and variable exploration within one search. This would create a
                        busier interface and restrict the specialised filters available for each record type. The better
                        solution is two separate searches—a familiar pattern for researchers—which allows Data Sources
                        &amp; Datasets Search to support discovery and Variables Search to support detailed comparison.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The trade-off is continuity. Researchers need to move repeatedly between the two searches
                        without
                        losing track of the datasets they have already considered. Only after narrowing the selection to
                        the most suitable dataset do they contact the responsible unit to request access.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <figure class="case-study-figure case-study-figure--userJourney">
                    <p class="figure-title handdrawn-underline">How researchers find suitable data?</p>
                    <Image src={userJourney} format="webp"
                           alt="simplified user journey showing the loop from dataset discovery to variables comparison and back"
                           class=""/>
                    <figcaption class="board-note">
                        Researchers may revisit their search and refine their selection
                        several times before identifying suitable datasets.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Carrying context between two searches</h2>

                    <p>
                        Keeping the searches separate protects the clarity of each interface but creates a continuity
                        problem. To bridge them, I proposed and designed <strong>Selected Datasets</strong>: a shared
                        selection carried between both contexts.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Researchers can gather promising datasets during discovery, use them to focus Variables Search,
                        and
                        return to refine their selection without starting again. The feature preserves the thread of the
                        investigation as they narrow a broad set of possibilities into a focused selection worth
                        pursuing.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <figure class="case-study-figure case-study-figure--selected-datasets">
                    <figcaption class="board-note">
                        Selected datasets carry over as filters in the variable search, allowing researchers to explore
                        and compare their variables without rebuilding their selection.
                    </figcaption>
                    <Image src={mexCarriedContext} format="webp"
                           alt="Focused crops of Data Sources &amp; Datasets Search and Variables Search,
                        showing that Selected Datasets persists between them."
                           class=""/>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Making dense metadata readable</h2>

                    <p>
                        Presenting MEx’s metadata as a flat reflection of the data model would make it difficult to
                        understand what matters. I designed reusable cards that group information by meaning and use
                        human-friendly labels. Long lists and multi-value fields initially show a short preview, with
                        the
                        full content available through <strong>Show more</strong>.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <figure class="case-study-figure case-study-figure--metadata-cards">
                    <Image src={missingData} format="webp"
                           alt="Focused comparison of a record with missing metadata hidden and revealed."
                           class=""/>
                    <figcaption class="board-note">
                        <strong>What’s missing matters. </strong>
                        Researchers can choose to reveal all metadata fields supported by MEx, including those without
                        values for the current record. This helps them distinguish between metadata the system does not
                        support and information missing from a particular record, while keeping the default view
                        uncluttered.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <p>
                        Core records — datasets, projects and publications — have full landing pages. Supporting
                        records, such as people and organisational units, open in a drawer with its own navigation
                        history.
                        Researchers can inspect related information without leaving the record they are evaluating or
                        losing the context of their investigation.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The difference between these behaviours also needed to be predictable. Navigation to a core
                        record
                        uses a semantic link, while opening a drawer is an action and uses a button. Styling every
                        action as
                        a conventional button would have overwhelmed pages containing many related records.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I retained the correct semantics in the markup and created a visual system for core-record
                        links.
                        Each is accompanied by a record-type tag with a distinctive icon and label; the same tags appear
                        in
                        search results and at the top of landing pages. Sighted users receive a consistent cue about the
                        destination, while screen-reader users encounter the semantic distinction between navigation and
                        an
                        in-page action.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <figure class="case-study-figure case-study-figure--record-relationships">
                    <Image src={recordsRelationship} format="webp"
                           alt="Supporting-record drawer beside a core-record link and its repeated
                        record-type tag."
                           class=""/>
                    <figcaption class="board-note">
                        Supporting records are shown in a supporting record drawer. Core records have their full landing
                        page and open in a new tab.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Turning findings into a prioritised roadmap</h2>

                    <p>
                        After the first implementation phase, I conducted a moderated UX evaluation with a researcher
                        who
                        had not used MEx before. I observed them completing two realistic dataset-discovery tasks. The
                        session confirmed the iterative movement between datasets and variables and exposed issues
                        across
                        metadata visibility, discoverability, language and search behaviour.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I documented each finding with a proposed response and assessed its expected impact. I then
                        reviewed
                        the recommendations with the development team, estimated implementation effort and mapped them
                        on
                        an impact–effort matrix. This gave us a shared basis for deciding what to deliver first.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <figure class="case-study-figure case-study-figure--evaluation-priorities">
                    <Image src={improvements} format="webp"
                           alt="The prioritisation framework with examples of implemented findings"
                           class=""/>
                    <figcaption class="board-note">
                        All improvements offering high impact for low effort have already been released, with work on
                        the remaining prioritised findings underway.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <p>
                        Several high-impact, low-effort improvements were implemented first. Because the participant
                        focused
                        on population, geographical and temporal coverage, we added this information directly to dataset
                        cards. A new control makes missing metadata explicit without overwhelming the default record
                        view,
                        and we reduced the prominence of a global search that had been confused with the specialised
                        searches.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        For the more complex search improvements, I led a workshop to translate broad expectations into
                        implementable matching and ranking rules—for example, whether a partial match in a title should
                        rank
                        above an exact match in a description.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Designing it - and making it real</h2>

                    <p>
                        MEx is built on InvenioRDM, but its specialist metadata model and research journey required
                        substantial adaptation. My development background helped me recognise platform constraints
                        early,
                        translate requirements into technical decisions and specify behaviour the team could build.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I annotated interaction behaviour, accessible labels, semantic HTML decisions and edge cases so
                        that the intended experience remained clear during development.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <figure class="case-study-figure case-study-figure--design-to-implementation">
                    <Image src={annotations} format="webp"
                           alt=""
                           class="annotations"
                            style="margin-bottom: 1rem"/>
                    <Image src={settings} format="webp"
                           alt=""
                           class=""/>
                    <div class="image-placeholder sr-only">
                        Annotated handover prototype, a short syntax-highlighted configuration
                        extract and the resulting rendered metadata card.
                    </div>
                    <figcaption class="board-note">
                        To carry the design through to the finished product, I provided the development team with
                        detailed handover annotations and implemented most of the user interface myself.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <p>
                        I implemented all of the styling and the complete record-page interface. A colleague implemented
                        the search functionality from my designs, while other team members worked on the backend and
                        infrastructure.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I also translated the record-page design system into maintainable configuration. Card groupings,
                        labels, icons and display rules are defined centrally, allowing the interface to evolve
                        alongside the metadata model without duplicating decisions across templates.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section easy-read">
                <h2>An ongoing partnership</h2>

                <p>
                    The features described here are implemented, and MEx continues to evolve. Before the initial
                    contract ended, RKI commissioned further development. At the end of 2025, the partnership continued
                    with a three-year agreement covering maintenance and new features. I remain involved as new
                    requirements and research needs emerge.
                </p>
            </section>

        </article>
        <img
                src={bg.src}
                alt=""
                class="background"
        />
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
